# The prod home, as a set of overrides. Source it; do not run it.
#
#   source infra/prod.env && ./infra/provision.sh a
#
# **Why a file and not a second config.sh.** `config.sh` already says every
# name in it is overridable from the environment, and README.md's "when a name
# is taken" already tells you to export for the WHOLE session rather than one
# command — because the failure it guards against is a home half in one project
# and half in another, which happens exactly when somebody re-runs one script
# with an override and the next one without. This file is that export block,
# written down once so it cannot be typed differently the second time.
#
# Everything here is `${VAR:-default}` so an explicit export still wins: the
# one-run exception below depends on that.
#
# ---- what is different about prod, and why ----

# A second project, not a second service in the first one. The blast radius of
# a mistake stops at a project boundary, and the dev home is where mistakes are
# supposed to happen. Globally unique and permanent, like every project id.
export ISOCAN_GCP_PROJECT="${ISOCAN_GCP_PROJECT:-isocan-io-prod}"

# The apex, not a subdomain. This is the address Scene 0 puts on a front page
# and the address `isocan setup` writes into a fresh machine's config — see
# `DEFAULT_HOME_URL` in packages/core.
export ISOCAN_DOMAIN="${ISOCAN_DOMAIN:-isocan.io}"

# **One instance, always.** Dev scales to zero so every dev request cold-boots
# through the crash-recovery path; prod holds one so a parked agent's socket is
# not hung up on, and so the GC timer's hourly tick is the ordinary rhythm
# rather than something the boot sweep has to stand in for. It is also the
# single largest line on the bill — ~$48/month, whether or not anyone visits.
export ISOCAN_MIN_INSTANCES="${ISOCAN_MIN_INSTANCES:-1}"

# **The meter's bypass, closed** (phase 13.7's open finding, decided in 14).
# The *.run.app URL stops answering entirely; the load balancer is the only
# door, and every request therefore arrives with a forwarded chain the
# infrastructure appended rather than one a caller made up.
#
# THE ONE-RUN EXCEPTION, and the only ordering trap in this file: a brand-new
# prod home has no load balancer yet, so there is no door for this to leave
# open. Stage A must be run once with the ingress open —
#
#     source infra/prod.env
#     ISOCAN_INGRESS=all ./infra/provision.sh a     # first time only
#     ./infra/provision.sh b                        # the load balancer + cert
#     # ... add the DNS A record, wait for the cert ...
#     ./infra/70-cloud-run.sh                       # now locks the ingress
#
# — and 70-cloud-run.sh is idempotent, so that last line is an ordinary re-run
# rather than a repair. It checks the DOMAIN instead of the run.app URL
# whenever this is not `all`, and refuses rather than shrugging if the domain
# does not answer.
export ISOCAN_INGRESS="${ISOCAN_INGRESS:-internal-and-cloud-load-balancing}"

# **Promotion is a gesture, not a branch.** Dev deploys whatever CI marks
# `green`; prod deploys the commit somebody tagged:
#
#     git tag -f prod green && git push -f origin prod
#
# `green`, not `main` — only a commit CI has tested may be promoted.
# 95-build-trigger.sh carries the full argument.
export ISOCAN_TRIGGER_TAG="${ISOCAN_TRIGGER_TAG:-^prod$}"
export ISOCAN_TRIGGER_NAME="${ISOCAN_TRIGGER_NAME:-isocan-prod-deploy}"

# ---- what is deliberately the SAME ----
#
# Region, service name, service-account names, the Artifact Registry repo, the
# LB resource names, max-instances, cpu, memory, concurrency, the request
# timeout. They are project-scoped, so `isocan-ip` in isocan-io-prod is a
# different address from `isocan-ip` in isocan-io-dev, and keeping the names
# identical is what makes every command in README.md work in either home with
# one variable changed. The bucket and the backup bucket derive from the
# project id, so they moved when it did.
#
# `ISOCAN_PROXY_HOPS` is NOT set here. The code's default of 1 is Google's
# documented behaviour for an external ALB, and the honest way to change it is
# to measure the real chain against this home first — see config.sh.
